### How do multiple catch blocks work?

Multiple catch blocks follow a single try block, ordered from most specific to most general. The JVM evaluates them top-to-bottom and executes the first one that matches the thrown exception.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MultipleCatchDemo {

    public static void processFile(int scenario) throws IOException {
        switch (scenario) {
            case 1 -> throw new FileNotFoundException("trades.csv not found");
            case 2 -> throw new IOException("Disk read error");
            case 3 -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Bad trade format");
            case 4 -> throw new NullPointerException("Null account reference");
        }
        System.out.println("Processed successfully");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            try {
                processFile(i);
            } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
                System.out.println("Scenario " + i + ": File not found - " + e.getMessage());
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.out.println("Scenario " + i + ": IO error - " + e.getMessage());
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                System.out.println("Scenario " + i + ": Runtime error - " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Scenario 1: File not found - trades.csv not found
Scenario 2: IO error - Disk read error
Scenario 3: Runtime error - Bad trade format
Scenario 4: Runtime error - Null account reference
Processed successfully
```

### Why must specific exceptions come before general ones?

Because catch blocks are checked top-to-bottom and the first match wins. If a general type like `Exception` comes first, it matches everything, making subsequent catches unreachable. The compiler rejects this as a compile error.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CatchOrderDemo {

    // WRONG: general before specific (compile error)
    // public static void wrongOrder() {
    //     try {
    //         throw new java.io.IOException("error");
    //     } catch (Exception e) {           // catches EVERYTHING
    //         System.out.println("General");
    //     } catch (java.io.IOException e) {  // UNREACHABLE!
    //         System.out.println("Specific");
    //     }
    //     // Compiler error: exception IOException has already been caught
    // }

    // CORRECT: specific before general
    public static void correctOrder() {
        try {
            throw new java.io.IOException("Disk error reading SAV2001 statement");
        } catch (java.io.FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Most specific: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (java.io.IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Less specific: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Catch-all: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        correctOrder();
    }
}
```

```text
Less specific: Disk error reading SAV2001 statement
```

### What is multi-catch (Java 7+)?

Multi-catch lets a single catch block handle multiple unrelated exception types using the pipe (`|`) operator. It eliminates duplicate code when two separate catch blocks do the same thing.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class MultiCatchDemo {

    // BEFORE Java 7: duplicate catch blocks
    public static void beforeMultiCatch() {
        try {
            throw new IOException("File error");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Old: export failed - " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Old: export failed - " + e.getMessage());  // DUPLICATE!
        }
    }

    // AFTER Java 7: multi-catch eliminates duplication
    public static void afterMultiCatch() {
        try {
            throw new IOException("File error");
        } catch (IOException | RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("New: export failed - " + e.getMessage());
            // e is implicitly final in multi-catch
            // e = new IOException("x");  // COMPILE ERROR: e is final
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        beforeMultiCatch();
        afterMultiCatch();
    }
}
```

```text
Old: export failed - File error
New: export failed - File error
```

### What are the rules for multi-catch?

The types in a multi-catch must be unrelated (no parent-child relationship), and the caught variable is implicitly final.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class MultiCatchRulesDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // VALID: unrelated exception types
        try {
            throw new IOException("test");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Valid multi-catch: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // INVALID: parent-child relationship
        // catch (IOException | FileNotFoundException e) { }  // COMPILE ERROR!
        // FileNotFoundException IS-A IOException, already caught by IOException.

        // Multi-catch variable is implicitly final:
        try {
            throw new IOException("test");
        } catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
            // e = new IOException("replaced");  // COMPILE ERROR: final
            System.out.println("Cannot reassign e: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Single catch variable is NOT final:
        try {
            throw new IOException("original");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e = new IOException("replaced");  // Allowed in single catch
            System.out.println("Reassigned in single catch: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Valid multi-catch: IOException
Cannot reassign e: test
Reassigned in single catch: replaced
```

### What are common mistakes with multiple catch blocks?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CatchMistakesDemo {

    // WRONG: catching Exception first makes all others unreachable
    // try { ... }
    // catch (Exception e) { }      // catches everything
    // catch (IOException e) { }    // COMPILE ERROR: unreachable

    // WRONG: catching Throwable hides Errors
    // try { ... }
    // catch (Throwable t) { }      // catches OOM, SOE, and all Exceptions

    // CORRECT: specific to general ordering
    public static void correctPattern() {
        try {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Bad account: SAV-INVALID");
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("1. Most specific: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("2. Less specific: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("3. Catch-all: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        correctPattern();
    }
}
```

```text
1. Most specific: Bad account: SAV-INVALID
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Bytecode efficiency.** Multi-catch compiles to a single handler that multiple exception-table rows point to. This is slightly more efficient than separate catch blocks with identical bodies.
- **Pattern matching for switch (Java 21).** Modern Java can switch on exception types, but catch blocks remain the standard mechanism for exception handling.
- **@ExceptionHandler in Spring.** Spring's global handler resolves exception types using IS-A matching, similar to catch block ordering. More specific handlers take priority.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How many catch blocks can follow a single try? (No limit, but must be ordered correctly.)
- Can you catch the same exception type twice? (No, the second is unreachable.)
- Why is the multi-catch variable final? (Compiler cannot know the concrete type, so reassignment would be unsafe.)
- Can multi-catch have more than two types? (`catch (A | B | C e)` is valid.)
